> "Joseph N. Hall" <remid0d...@gmail.com> wrote:

>> The "XY problem" is someone asking how to do X
>> because he actually wants to do Y, but X is the
>> wrong way to do it.

> I happen to have a problem Y that I would like
> fixed: to me, infinite generation feels inelegant.

This is a _game_. Elegance is absolutely a "bottom
of the list" consideration in game design. Consider
that this game can still be crashed just by stacking
"a lot" of items on a square. That is a "top of the
list" problem for consideration. Most game software
is so incredibly ugly "behind the curtain" just to
save a few CPU cycles per screen frame refresh, it
make those who program "for elegance" want to vomit
when they read that code.

That's pretty much the general effect of reading
NetHack code anyway.

There's a real moral lesson in the naming of the
"Hack" part of "NetHack"; this isn't meant to be
coding where elegance is a prime consideration.

> [fuzzy but good way to cure "infinite" generation,
> by making it "finite but large" generation
> instead]

The waste of effort that this whole line of thinking
incorporates is that "infinite" is being bandied
about, but "infinite" is never the case: a player
will die of old age long before "infinite" is
reached. So will the universe. What farming provides
is "large quantities of goodies for the large amount
of time a patient player will be willing to invest",
FULL STOP.

The end result is that changes that simply put a
limit that no one ever reaches anyway on the number
of puddings created are a large waste of effort.

The creation of puddings is already self limited by
player patience, and for most pudding farmers, much
less than those oppose pudding farming realize.

This farming time is usually _much less_ than the
time an extinctionist will invest in a game of
NetHack, but then, the goal is different.

Why so many people find "high return on investment"
offensive baffles me. That is the measure of merit
for every kind of money management and every form of
business management ever praised, and arguably, is
the best way to approach _all_ of NetHack play, not
just the parts susceptible to patience play.

Quantum valeat.

